<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Grand Duchy of Posen</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1848 CE · capital Poznan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Prussian province with limited Polish self-rule, created at the Congress of Vienna and stripped of its autonomy after the revolution of 1848.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 the western part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-warsaw">Duchy of Warsaw</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznan</a>, was returned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>. To reconcile its mainly Polish population, it was given the title of grand duchy and a promise of respect for Polish language and institutions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> ruled through a Polish governor, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoni-radziwill">Antoni Radziwill</a>, and the Polish language was used in schools and courts. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> of 1830 in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/congress-poland">Congress Poland</a>, the provincial president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-flottwell">Eduard Flottwell</a> began a policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a>. Polish landowners and clergy led a lively cultural and economic life, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazar-poznan">Bazar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwik-mieroslawski">Ludwik Mieroslawski</a> led a Polish uprising in the grand duchy, which Prussian troops crushed. Its special status was abolished, and it became the ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-posen">Province of Posen</a>. In 1919 most of it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/grand-duchy-of-posen.jpg" alt="Grand Duchy of Posen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Posen_(1815).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1848 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Poznan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antoni Radziwill, Frederick William III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Grand Duchy of Poznan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1832&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-posen" aria-label="Grand Duchy of Posen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5060 369 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Grand Duchy of Posen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5380 625L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Poznan. <a href="/atlas?year=1832&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-posen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

